That's a csh messge, not a perl message.  I suspect the script isn't
coming anywhere near perl.  The kernel isn't interpreting the

    #!/bnr/contrib/bin/perl

line correctly, and when the exec fails, the shell is trying to guess
how to run the script, and guessing wrong because it thinks that
an initial # means to run under csh, being stupid about the #! magic
number.

There are any number of reasons why the exec might be failing.  That
directory might not be where perl lives, or the permissions on perl
might be wrong.  If the path is a symlink to a pathname longer than 32
chars, some systems won't deal with it.  Try copying perl into
/tmp, and change the shebang line to #!/tmp/perl.  If the script then
gets past your csh message, this probably indicates a pathname longer
than 32 chars somewhere.

By the way, you've got version 4.019--the latest is 4.036, which may
(or may not) work better on the script in question.

